Despite their distance from the live wire,

248
00:14:30,201 --> 00:14:34,000
electricity traveled more than 200 feet through pooled water,

249
00:14:34,001 --> 00:14:34,800
killing them instantly.

250
00:14:36,900 --> 00:14:40,940
No one saw it coming, yet the results were all too familiar.

251
00:14:43,440 --> 00:14:45,580
Electric power lines kill.

252
00:14:46,940 --> 00:14:49,340
No one is more aware of that than Spider Lockhart,

253
00:14:50,540 --> 00:14:53,740
who spends his days more than 200 feet above the ground,

254
00:14:54,560 --> 00:14:56,500
repairing the biggest power lines of all.

255
00:15:00,020 --> 00:15:03,140
For him and other linesmen at Haverfield Corporation,

256
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:07,100
Working on 500,000 volts is all in a day's work.

257
00:15:08,940 --> 00:15:11,500
It will feel like little ants crawling around

258
00:15:11,501 --> 00:15:14,100
between the electrical connection of your gloves

259
00:15:14,101 --> 00:15:15,980
to your sleeve of your jacket,

260
00:15:16,240 --> 00:15:17,980
or sometimes even around your neck,

261
00:15:18,500 --> 00:15:19,920
and you'll get to scratching them

262
00:15:19,921 --> 00:15:21,760
just because you think something's on there,

263
00:15:22,180 --> 00:15:25,160
and all it is is just a stray voltage going across you.

264
00:15:26,100 --> 00:15:28,820
High-voltage power lines are the electric lifeblood

265
00:15:28,821 --> 00:15:30,080
of America's power grid.

266
00:15:31,500 --> 00:15:33,040
Lose a line for even a few hours,

267
00:15:33,041 --> 00:15:35,800
and more than customer inconvenience is at stake.

268
00:15:36,900 --> 00:15:38,820
So too are millions of dollars.

269
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:41,700
That's where Haverfield comes in,

270
00:15:42,280 --> 00:15:47,580
because they can get to even remote locations faster and more efficiently than any ground team.

271
00:15:49,260 --> 00:15:53,140
The beauty of what we do is we can go right directly to the conductor

272
00:15:53,141 --> 00:15:57,080
or to the problem site and access it immediately.

273
00:15:58,040 --> 00:16:01,740
Once we finish one, we can fly to the next one, so that's very speedy.

274
00:16:03,040 --> 00:16:10,180
It all makes sense, except 90% of all outdoor power lines are not insulated, and they can kill you.

275
00:16:12,320 --> 00:16:16,160
The only defense? Experience and the proper tools.

276
00:16:16,161 --> 00:16:20,320
What I've got here is what we call a hot suit.

277
00:16:21,560 --> 00:16:26,660
And a hot suit is a steel cage that encloses the linemen.

278
00:16:26,661 --> 00:16:31,660
Made of 25% steel thread and 75% Nomex,

279
00:16:31,760 --> 00:16:35,660
a fire retardant material, the hot suit forms a protective metal

280
00:16:35,760 --> 00:16:39,980
shell around the linesman, just like the aluminum shell

281
00:16:40,080 --> 00:16:44,880
of an airliner and another example of the skin effect.

282
00:16:44,980 --> 00:16:47,420
When they put that on, the electricity itself

283
00:16:47,520 --> 00:16:49,720
will flow over the lineman instead of going

284
00:16:49,820 --> 00:16:52,680
to the lineman itself, so we can safely work

285
00:16:52,780 --> 00:16:53,780
on these high voltages.

286
00:16:56,660 --> 00:17:00,160
Once set, it's time to fly.

287
00:17:00,161 --> 00:17:06,000
We'll fly about 30 to 40 miles an hour down the line.

288
00:17:06,001 --> 00:17:08,300
These are fast patrols, and what they do,

289
00:17:08,301 --> 00:17:16,120
they will visually see any major problems that that line may have.

290
00:17:16,121 --> 00:17:19,020
The relationship between linesman and helicopter pilot

291
00:17:19,021 --> 00:17:24,220
must be one of absolute trust, and communication is essential.

292
00:17:24,221 --> 00:17:27,020
We are attached together on that platform,

293
00:17:27,260 --> 00:17:29,540
whether I'm standing on the skid or on the platform itself.

294
00:17:29,660 --> 00:17:32,100
So if anything goes wrong, it's both of us in it together.

295
00:17:32,880 --> 00:17:34,460
So do we trust each other? Yes.

296
00:17:36,680 --> 00:17:39,300
The biggest danger is an electric booby trap,

297
00:17:39,760 --> 00:17:41,060
invisible to the human eye.

298
00:17:43,800 --> 00:17:46,420
As an electric charge races through the power line,

299
00:17:46,421 --> 00:17:48,320
it builds up electric potential,

300
00:17:49,360 --> 00:17:52,520
creating electromagnetic fields that envelop the line like a halo.

301
00:17:54,220 --> 00:17:57,320
Stay outside the fields and the danger is minimal.

302
00:17:57,420 --> 00:18:00,160
But get too close, and the electricity will

303
00:18:00,260 --> 00:18:03,000
leap like a lightning bolt, and sometimes

304
00:18:03,100 --> 00:18:04,100
with deadly consequences.

305
00:18:07,240 --> 00:18:09,560
Where that energized wire is, you

306
00:18:09,660 --> 00:18:12,300
have to be a minimum of 12 feet away from it.

307
00:18:12,400 --> 00:18:16,540
Otherwise, that electricity will jump over to you.

308
00:18:16,640 --> 00:18:20,720
And if you're close to it, then that will kill you.

309
00:18:20,820 --> 00:18:22,380
To prevent this from happening,

310
00:18:22,381 --> 00:18:25,980
The crews at Haverfield use a simple metal rod to draw and

311
00:18:26,080 --> 00:18:27,780
control the potentially deadly arm.

312
00:18:31,080 --> 00:18:34,720
The way we do that is we take our rod and once we get inside

313
00:18:34,820 --> 00:18:38,960
that field, that 12 feet, we have that rod extended and once

314
00:18:39,060 --> 00:18:42,460
it gets close enough, the electrical connection between

315
00:18:42,560 --> 00:18:45,700
that wire and the platform will jump to the end of the rod.

316
00:18:48,740 --> 00:18:51,380
Once the copter gets close enough, the linesman

317
00:18:51,381 --> 00:18:54,880
the rod to the power line, minimizing the arc,

318
00:18:54,881 --> 00:18:57,220
and allowing the line's electromagnetic fields

319
00:18:57,221 --> 00:19:00,220
to race across and envelop the copter and linesman.

320
00:19:02,720 --> 00:19:05,220
And from that point on, we are energized

321
00:19:05,221 --> 00:19:08,220
at a half a million volts.

322
00:19:08,221 --> 00:19:10,840
Once energized, Spyder attaches a clamp

323
00:19:10,841 --> 00:19:14,140
known as a platform bond to provide a more secure connection

324
00:19:14,141 --> 00:19:16,840
than the rod.

325
00:19:16,841 --> 00:19:19,380
If the helicopter needs to pull off,

326
00:19:19,381 --> 00:19:22,380
for whatever reason, everything is breakaway.

327
00:19:22,381 --> 00:19:26,380
We do not attach ourselves permanently to the wire itself.

328
00:19:26,381 --> 00:19:29,380
So once I have this attached to the conductor itself,

329
00:19:29,381 --> 00:19:32,380
I can lay the rod down and I can proceed to go to work from that point on.

330
00:19:35,380 --> 00:19:39,380
You get out there and you would think being out in the middle of nowhere,

331
00:19:39,381 --> 00:19:42,380
it would be very pristine and quiet. It is not.

332
00:19:42,381 --> 00:19:44,380
It is very noisy up there.

333
00:19:44,381 --> 00:19:47,380
Once you're sitting there energizing half a million volts,

334
00:19:47,381 --> 00:19:54,020
what you hear is that feel of electricity flowing around that wire, and it is noisy.

335
00:19:55,440 --> 00:19:58,840
Out on the line, the linesman performs detailed inspections,

336
00:19:59,180 --> 00:20:01,200
as well as any necessary repair work.

337
00:20:04,340 --> 00:20:08,340
And that bag the linesman brings with him not only holds his equipment,

338
00:20:09,020 --> 00:20:13,800
but also a rope ladder system, should he become stranded or need to evacuate quickly.

339
00:20:13,801 --> 00:20:18,800
But does this mean the entire process is shockproof?

340
00:20:18,900 --> 00:20:23,280
Sure, so long as everything goes according to plan.

341
00:20:23,380 --> 00:20:25,140
Sometimes it doesn't.

342
00:20:27,340 --> 00:20:30,980
In 1988, Spider's metal rod fell,

343
00:20:31,080 --> 00:20:34,060
and it took an immediate electric shock to the leg.

344
00:20:34,160 --> 00:20:38,160
It came to 24 exit wounds and stopped my heart.

345
00:20:38,260 --> 00:20:40,360
Once they got me on the ground, I

346
00:20:40,460 --> 00:20:43,800
had a gentleman that performed CPR on me.

347
00:20:43,801 --> 00:20:47,940
and got my heart back started.

348
00:20:48,040 --> 00:20:50,100
I get scared.

349
00:20:50,200 --> 00:20:52,100
If you're not scared, you're not safe.

350
00:20:54,840 --> 00:20:57,640
Bravery and training may be the keys for electric linesmen.

351
00:21:00,460 --> 00:21:03,260
But at prisons across the United States,

352
00:21:03,360 --> 00:21:04,860
not even the most hardened prisoner

353
00:21:04,960 --> 00:21:07,400
will risk a ride on these shocking wires.

354
00:21:10,600 --> 00:21:12,460
According to the National Safety Council,

355
00:21:12,461 --> 00:21:15,260
Your odds of being killed by an electrical transmission line

356
00:21:15,261 --> 00:21:19,100
during one calendar year are approximately 3 million to 1.

357
00:21:19,760 --> 00:21:24,260
Over the course of your lifetime, the odds diminish to about 40,000 to 1.

358
00:21:25,180 --> 00:21:27,780
Most shocking will return on Modern Marvels.

359
00:21:30,380 --> 00:21:35,200
There is no more ominous symbol of electricity's power than the electric chair.

360
00:21:37,940 --> 00:21:41,840
To date, more than 4,000 people have absorbed its shocking power.

361
00:21:42,460 --> 00:21:47,360
And only one, convicted murderer Willie Francis,

362
00:21:47,361 --> 00:21:50,000
who benefited from an incompetent electrician,

363
00:21:50,400 --> 00:21:51,380
lived to tell about it.

364
00:21:54,060 --> 00:21:56,240
Today, the chair is rarely used.

365
00:21:56,980 --> 00:21:58,620
But that doesn't mean electric shock

366
00:21:58,621 --> 00:22:01,160
doesn't still play an integral role in prison life.

367
00:22:04,580 --> 00:22:07,800
Kern Valley State Prison, Delano, California.

368
00:22:09,460 --> 00:22:11,620
Home to 5,000 prisoners.

369
00:22:12,320 --> 00:22:14,100
nearly all of whom are level four,

370
00:22:14,740 --> 00:22:17,180
max security, the worst of the worst.

371
00:22:18,320 --> 00:22:21,240
But if you expect to see armed officers in every tower,

372
00:22:21,680 --> 00:22:22,440
you'd be mistaken.

373
00:22:23,060 --> 00:22:24,100
That's old school.

374
00:22:25,360 --> 00:22:27,900
Today, the best deterrent doesn't come at the end of a gun,

375
00:22:28,340 --> 00:22:31,980
but rather through the shocking power of this faceless killer,

376
00:22:32,900 --> 00:22:34,560
a lethal electrified fence.

377
00:22:36,300 --> 00:22:38,720
Touch it once, and you're a goner.

378
00:22:38,721 --> 00:22:43,360
What makes a fence lethal is not necessarily the voltage.

379
00:22:44,180 --> 00:22:49,380
Like static electricity can put 10,000 volts by rubbing your feet on the rug and you touch a doorknob.

380
00:22:49,940 --> 00:22:53,860
What makes it lethal is a large amount of current passing through the body.

381
00:22:54,200 --> 00:22:59,080
70 milliamps, 71 thousandths of an amp is typically considered lethal.

382
00:22:59,520 --> 00:23:04,660
This puts out one and a half amps, which is 20 times the lethal dose.

383
00:23:04,661 --> 00:23:08,600
Electrified fencing has been used as a lethal deterrent

384
00:23:08,601 --> 00:23:09,720
since World War I.

385
00:23:11,040 --> 00:23:13,980
In that era, nothing stood between the deadly wires

386
00:23:13,981 --> 00:23:15,560
and those that dared to go near it.

387
00:23:17,700 --> 00:23:20,660
Recent decades have seen increased demand for more prisons,

388
00:23:21,560 --> 00:23:24,280
and with it, renewed interest in the shocking power

389
00:23:24,281 --> 00:23:25,400
of electrified fences.

390
00:23:27,000 --> 00:23:31,240
In the 1980s, the California Department of Corrections

391
00:23:31,241 --> 00:23:34,400
and Rehabilitation went on an expanded building program

392
00:23:34,401 --> 00:23:38,140
We went from 12 facilities to now we have an excess of 30.

393
00:23:38,141 --> 00:23:40,840
In doing so, we looked at savings everywhere

394
00:23:40,841 --> 00:23:42,940
and increasing security.

395
00:23:43,300 --> 00:23:46,740
And it was suggested to use an electrified fence.

396
00:23:48,020 --> 00:23:51,220
No longer are armed officers required in every tower,

397
00:23:51,920 --> 00:23:52,940
saving the state millions

398
00:23:52,941 --> 00:23:55,800
while never letting anyone forget the stakes.

399
00:23:56,680 --> 00:23:58,560
So if you brush up against it,

400
00:23:58,620 --> 00:23:59,760
even just a little bit of a touch,

401
00:24:00,540 --> 00:24:01,580
you've as good as touched it,

402
00:24:01,600 --> 00:24:02,860
and you get that lethal dose.

403
00:24:04,400 --> 00:24:08,200
The Delano fence features 16 wires supported by 12,000

404
00:24:08,201 --> 00:24:11,240
volt insulators on posts of galvanized steel.

405
00:24:14,040 --> 00:24:17,040
Woven nylon mesh surrounds the lower nine wires

406
00:24:17,041 --> 00:24:20,880
to deter birds and other animals.

407
00:24:20,881 --> 00:24:23,520
The middle posts are grounded, both with the foundation

408
00:24:23,521 --> 00:24:26,400
and with a copper ground wire to make sure that if there's

409
00:24:26,401 --> 00:24:30,620
any contact between the wire and a person and the fence

410
00:24:30,621 --> 00:24:34,160
post, that we have a good conductive path.

411
00:24:34,161 --> 00:24:37,200
Each wire is made of six strands of stainless steel

412
00:24:37,201 --> 00:24:43,000
woven over a copper core, which increases conductivity.

413
00:24:43,001 --> 00:24:45,700
Not surprisingly, access to this no man's land

414
00:24:45,701 --> 00:24:49,280
is strictly supervised, and a tightly controlled procedure

415
00:24:49,281 --> 00:24:54,320
is undertaken to gain access, even if it were to rescue

416
00:24:54,321 --> 00:24:57,280
a potential escapee.

417
00:24:57,281 --> 00:24:58,820
What we're getting ready to do is we're getting ready

418
00:24:58,821 --> 00:25:00,420
to de-energize the electrified fence.

419
00:25:00,421 --> 00:25:02,880
That's what we call when we take the fence down,

420
00:25:02,881 --> 00:25:04,800
so staff can go inside the fence line.

421
00:25:05,200 --> 00:25:07,260
So at this time, I'm going to ask for authorization

422
00:25:07,261 --> 00:25:08,500
from the watch commander.

423
00:25:09,580 --> 00:25:12,620
Only after armed officers confirm the area is clear

424
00:25:12,621 --> 00:25:15,100
does the watch commander give the okay.

425
00:25:16,300 --> 00:25:18,660
Finishing up now. All zones clear.

426
00:25:19,120 --> 00:25:21,400
Copy. Is the fence check complete?

427
00:25:22,120 --> 00:25:23,560
10-4. All zones clear.

428
00:25:25,160 --> 00:25:27,100
Like something out of Mission Impossible,

429
00:25:27,780 --> 00:25:31,940
a series of switches must be turned an exact sequence

430
00:25:31,941 --> 00:25:35,440
in order to power down the fence and release the key to the gate.

431
00:25:39,620 --> 00:25:43,380
And for security reasons, that sequence remains highly confidential.

432
00:25:44,760 --> 00:25:46,640
If you've done it and done it correctly,

433
00:25:46,820 --> 00:25:48,460
you will be able to retrieve the key,

434
00:25:48,900 --> 00:25:52,720
which is encapsulated always inside here for just this entire section.

435
00:25:54,360 --> 00:25:56,140
And even in a potential escape,

436
00:25:56,141 --> 00:26:00,480
a final voltage test is required to confirm each wire is de-energized.

437
00:26:01,940 --> 00:26:06,180
That may seem like a lot of steps, but make no mistake.

438
00:26:06,280 --> 00:26:10,020
With this deadly technology, there are no second chances.

439
00:26:10,120 --> 00:26:13,080
And so far, not even the most hardened criminals

440
00:26:13,180 --> 00:26:14,880
have been willing to risk it.

441
00:26:14,980 --> 00:26:17,020
Prior to the electrified fence,

442
00:26:17,120 --> 00:26:19,360
there were occasions that inmates had escaped

443
00:26:19,460 --> 00:26:23,360
over the fences with towers in place.

444
00:26:23,460 --> 00:26:26,160
Since the electrified fences have been installed,

445
00:26:26,260 --> 00:26:28,260
there's not one successful escape

446
00:26:28,360 --> 00:26:31,940
through an electrified fence in California as of this date.

447
00:26:31,941 --> 00:26:36,180
Few things rival a lethal fence's shocking power,

448
00:26:36,181 --> 00:26:41,060
and only one stands in plain sight for millions of commuters every day.

449
00:26:41,061 --> 00:26:44,260
It's known as the third rail.

450
00:26:44,261 --> 00:26:47,300
Right now we're riding the blue line.

451
00:26:47,301 --> 00:26:50,660
We just left the Frankfurt Transportation Center and we're heading towards Center City.

452
00:26:52,100 --> 00:26:57,940
The blue line is a third rail system that utilizes 600 volt DC to power our trains.

453
00:26:57,941 --> 00:27:02,140
As we head into the city now, you can see the rail system

454
00:27:02,240 --> 00:27:04,240
here to this side.

455
00:27:04,340 --> 00:27:06,140
Look down into any subway track,

456
00:27:06,240 --> 00:27:08,620
and the third rail is the one that parallels the two main

457
00:27:08,720 --> 00:27:11,260
tracks, providing power to the train.

458
00:27:13,280 --> 00:27:16,820
Put even a single foot on it, and odds are you're toast.

459
00:27:19,360 --> 00:27:22,640
Contact is made by a series of metal plates attached to each

460
00:27:22,740 --> 00:27:24,040
train car.

461
00:27:24,140 --> 00:27:27,000
These are known as shoes, and they slide along the bottom

462
00:27:27,001 --> 00:27:28,800
along the bottom face of the third rail.

463
00:27:32,000 --> 00:27:33,840
Most third rails are usually a combination

464
00:27:33,940 --> 00:27:35,500
of aluminum and steel.

465
00:27:35,600 --> 00:27:37,920
It has to be as big as it is, because we are pulling

466
00:27:38,020 --> 00:27:39,280
at times 10,000 amps.

467
00:27:39,380 --> 00:27:42,620
And if it were smaller, it would actually melt.

468
00:27:42,720 --> 00:27:44,880
As the shoes and rail touch, the shoe

469
00:27:44,980 --> 00:27:49,420
draws the current into the car's electrical system.

470
00:27:49,520 --> 00:27:51,120
We're underneath a market-Frankford subway

471
00:27:51,220 --> 00:27:54,560
elevator train, and this is how we get the power

472
00:27:54,660 --> 00:27:56,400
from the third rail.

473
00:27:56,401 --> 00:27:59,700
We take 600 volts through the third rail shoe.

474
00:27:59,800 --> 00:28:02,040
We bring it into our propulsion inverter,

475
00:28:02,140 --> 00:28:05,740
which then changes the DC power to AC power.

476
00:28:05,840 --> 00:28:08,540
And then we bring it back into an AC motor.

477
00:28:08,640 --> 00:28:10,040
And that's what gives our train the propulsion

478
00:28:10,140 --> 00:28:11,340
and takes it up and down the line.

479
00:28:16,360 --> 00:28:18,720
The power drives the motor as well as the lights

480
00:28:18,820 --> 00:28:23,200
and air conditioner, then returns back through the rails

481
00:28:23,300 --> 00:28:26,100
on which the train rides.

482
00:28:26,101 --> 00:28:29,380
In essence, a subway train is a simple, if massive, circuit.

483
00:28:30,500 --> 00:28:34,460
The positive side is the third rail system, and the negative is the rail.

484
00:28:34,740 --> 00:28:36,660
Our substation is basically the battery.

485
00:28:37,180 --> 00:28:39,860
The wire coming from the substation goes to the third rail.

486
00:28:40,300 --> 00:28:45,500
The train uses the power and then returns it through the wheels of the train back to the running rails.

487
00:28:45,501 --> 00:28:49,900
And the rails are connected with another cable back to the substation, so it completes a complete circuit.

488
00:28:51,320 --> 00:28:53,940
Most third rails do feature protective covering,

489
00:28:53,941 --> 00:28:57,340
but it's there to shield the rail from foreign material,

490
00:28:57,341 --> 00:28:59,580
not people from electrocution.

491
00:28:59,581 --> 00:29:03,580
Worst-case scenario, if you were to touch the rail

492
00:29:03,581 --> 00:29:06,880
and you were to grab hold, the voltage is so high on the system

493
00:29:06,881 --> 00:29:09,760
and the current can get so high that while your hands

494
00:29:09,761 --> 00:29:11,920
may only have minor burns, you're actually burning

495
00:29:11,921 --> 00:29:13,560
from the inside. It could burn your organs.

496
00:29:13,561 --> 00:29:15,260
It could cause internal injuries.

497
00:29:15,261 --> 00:29:18,200
So while you may not see a lot of injury on the outside,

498
00:29:18,201 --> 00:29:21,300
it'll actually be probleming you significantly on the inside.

499
00:29:21,301 --> 00:29:23,760
Electric shockers like the third rail

500
00:29:23,860 --> 00:29:26,840
may quickly end your life.

501
00:29:26,940 --> 00:29:29,700
But procedures like electroshock therapy

502
00:29:29,800 --> 00:29:33,640
can actually restart it.

503
00:29:33,740 --> 00:29:37,440
Since 1946, nearly two dozen New York City transit workers

504
00:29:37,540 --> 00:29:40,560
have been electrocuted by contact with the third rail

505
00:29:40,660 --> 00:29:44,960
across the city's 842 miles of subway system.

506
00:29:45,060 --> 00:29:47,860
Most shocking will return on Modern Marvels.

507
00:29:51,300 --> 00:30:00,460
In moments, you will witness an actual electroconvulsive therapy session,

508
00:30:01,240 --> 00:30:04,340
ECT, once known as Electroshock.

509
00:30:06,620 --> 00:30:09,720
Today, ECT is a leading form of medical treatment

510
00:30:09,721 --> 00:30:11,920
for people who suffer from severe depression.

511
00:30:13,800 --> 00:30:18,140
When people have exhausted either antidepressant drugs

512
00:30:18,141 --> 00:30:21,140
or they've just reached the point where they're so depleted

513
00:30:21,141 --> 00:30:24,520
by their depression, that they can no longer function

514
00:30:24,521 --> 00:30:25,900
or can no longer stand it.

515
00:30:26,760 --> 00:30:28,700
These are the people who benefit from ECT.

516
00:30:30,680 --> 00:30:33,860
ECT first rose to prominence in the 1930s.

517
00:30:34,840 --> 00:30:38,880
Some European psychiatrists had observed for decades

518
00:30:38,881 --> 00:30:42,360
that when depressed people had a spontaneous epileptic seizure,

519
00:30:42,960 --> 00:30:44,080
their depression got better.

520
00:30:44,860 --> 00:30:47,740
Using this insight, doctors began to induce seizures,

521
00:30:47,741 --> 00:30:51,160
first with chemicals, then with electroshock.

522
00:30:52,800 --> 00:30:55,280
During the 1950s and 60s,

523
00:30:55,800 --> 00:30:58,180
variations of electrotherapy were misused.

524
00:30:59,360 --> 00:31:02,300
But those historic abuses bear no relation

525
00:31:02,301 --> 00:31:04,220
to today's ECT procedures.

526
00:31:07,040 --> 00:31:09,520
Whenever I mention ECT,

527
00:31:09,780 --> 00:31:11,960
people immediately think of Jack Nicholson

528
00:31:11,961 --> 00:31:13,260
and One Flew Over the Cuckoo's Nest.

529
00:31:13,660 --> 00:31:15,080
It was a terrific performance,

530
00:31:15,081 --> 00:31:19,480
But it was an inaccurate depiction of what ECT is actually all about.

531
00:31:20,140 --> 00:31:23,880
In fact, it's a much more benign situation than what he depicted.

532
00:31:24,500 --> 00:31:26,260
Nice deep breath. You're doing great.

533
00:31:26,860 --> 00:31:32,580
During a typical ECT procedure, a dosage of only 30 to 50 joules of electric current is delivered.

534
00:31:33,780 --> 00:31:37,040
That's less shock power than is found in a 100-watt light bulb.

535
00:31:39,480 --> 00:31:43,320
These are the treatment electrodes that we use for ECT.

536
00:31:43,321 --> 00:31:49,320
They are placed on the head, one on the side of the head and one on the top of the head.

537
00:31:49,321 --> 00:31:51,320
It's not a lot of current.

538
00:31:51,321 --> 00:31:58,320
Occasionally when I run this through my hand, it feels like a tingling, but it's enough current to trigger a grand mal seizure.

539
00:31:58,321 --> 00:32:01,320
The electrical current does nothing therapeutic.

540
00:32:01,321 --> 00:32:08,320
It is simply the safest way we have to trigger a grand mal seizure under very controlled circumstances.

541
00:32:08,321 --> 00:32:14,540
We put cushions in the mouth so that there is no chance of injury to the teeth during

542
00:32:14,541 --> 00:32:16,580
the treatment.

543
00:32:16,581 --> 00:32:25,360
And now we'll give the treatment itself.

544
00:32:25,361 --> 00:32:27,820
You see a clenching of the jaw during the stimulus.

545
00:32:27,821 --> 00:32:33,000
The stimulus is now over and the patient is having a gromal seizure.

546
00:32:33,001 --> 00:32:36,440
We see slight movement in the hands and the feet,

547
00:32:36,441 --> 00:32:39,500
but the main part of the body is motionless

548
00:32:39,501 --> 00:32:40,400
during the treatment.

549
00:32:43,540 --> 00:32:47,240
Now, during ECT, the same thing happens

550
00:32:47,241 --> 00:32:49,780
as happens during an epileptic seizure.

551
00:32:49,781 --> 00:32:54,160
The entire brain goes on and off about three times

552
00:32:54,161 --> 00:32:58,600
per second for as much as a minute.

553
00:32:58,601 --> 00:33:02,660
We have no idea why this event, which

554
00:33:02,661 --> 00:33:07,160
is usually catastrophic for depressed people.

555
00:33:07,161 --> 00:33:12,120
Turns out to be therapeutic and sometimes even life-saving.

556
00:33:12,121 --> 00:33:15,220
Side effects include a brief intense headache

557
00:33:15,221 --> 00:33:17,540
and short-term memory loss.

558
00:33:17,541 --> 00:33:21,460
Long-term memories are not affected.

559
00:33:21,461 --> 00:33:25,540
That was my concern, that I would lose more of memory

560
00:33:25,541 --> 00:33:31,100
or something important that you can't buy.

561
00:33:32,660 --> 00:33:36,820
a childhood memory or my nieces and nephews or who I was.

562
00:33:36,821 --> 00:33:37,660
You awake?

563
00:33:38,740 --> 00:33:39,660
Yeah.

564
00:33:39,661 --> 00:33:42,060
I guess I can just say it was a journey for me

565
00:33:43,140 --> 00:33:48,140
to first accept, you know, where I was

566
00:33:49,860 --> 00:33:52,820
and to believe it could work.

567
00:33:54,580 --> 00:33:59,440
So I have, I'm grateful that it was there.

568
00:34:02,660 --> 00:34:06,300
You've seen these in almost every current TV medical drama.

569
00:34:07,100 --> 00:34:08,100
The paddles of life.

570
00:34:09,420 --> 00:34:12,060
Reversing heart failure through the power of electric shock.

571
00:34:13,260 --> 00:34:14,460
Get him on a monitor.

572
00:34:14,960 --> 00:34:15,660
CPR.

573
00:34:15,661 --> 00:34:18,060
Here's how they work when a life is on the line.

574
00:34:18,740 --> 00:34:19,820
Stop CPR.

575
00:34:19,980 --> 00:34:20,760
What's my rhythm?

576
00:34:21,220 --> 00:34:26,160
Seconds count because we know that the longer it takes to restart a heart and to establish

577
00:34:26,161 --> 00:34:30,420
an airway, the less likely it is that an individual will survive.

578
00:34:30,520 --> 00:34:31,760
Let's get pacer pads on.

579
00:34:31,761 --> 00:34:34,260
Today, those bulky paddles have been replaced

580
00:34:34,261 --> 00:34:36,760
by more efficient sticky patches.

581
00:34:36,761 --> 00:34:38,760
Yet the technology remains the same.

582
00:34:38,761 --> 00:34:40,760
I need lines. I need epi, please.

583
00:34:40,761 --> 00:34:43,760
It's called an external heart defibrillator,

584
00:34:43,761 --> 00:34:47,760
the first line of defense during cardiac arrest.

585
00:34:47,761 --> 00:34:49,760
Back, back, clear!

586
00:34:49,761 --> 00:34:51,760
Clear!

587
00:34:51,761 --> 00:34:54,760
The defibrillator is one of the integral components

588
00:34:54,761 --> 00:34:56,760
of the resuscitative effort,

589
00:34:56,761 --> 00:35:00,760
because without it, you have no way

590
00:35:00,761 --> 00:35:03,660
of restarting the heart and reestablishing

591
00:35:03,760 --> 00:35:07,600
the normal electrical function of the heart.

592
00:35:07,700 --> 00:35:10,900
A healthy heart has a natural built-in pacemaker

593
00:35:11,000 --> 00:35:13,800
that creates electric impulses, causing the heart to contract

594
00:35:13,900 --> 00:35:16,000
with a regular rhythm and pump the blood.

595
00:35:18,520 --> 00:35:22,620
But in cardiac arrest, those natural impulses become chaotic,

596
00:35:22,720 --> 00:35:25,160
and the heart begins to fail.

597
00:35:25,260 --> 00:35:28,560
Normally, when the heart is beating,

598
00:35:28,561 --> 00:35:31,800
It's a coordinated electrical conduction

599
00:35:31,900 --> 00:35:33,860
from the top to the bottom, followed

600
00:35:33,960 --> 00:35:36,040
by mechanical contraction.

601
00:35:36,140 --> 00:35:39,000
Very, very rhythmic and organized.

602
00:35:39,100 --> 00:35:43,940
When fibrillation occurs, any organization ceases,

603
00:35:44,040 --> 00:35:48,380
and the heart is not beating in an effective fashion

604
00:35:48,480 --> 00:35:52,380
and not moving blood out to the body.

605
00:35:52,480 --> 00:35:54,820
The two patches are positioned opposite one another,

606
00:35:54,920 --> 00:35:55,920
with the heart in the middle.

607
00:35:58,560 --> 00:36:01,120
and electric charges applied, creating a brief,

608
00:36:01,121 --> 00:36:03,700
intense electric circuit.

609
00:36:03,701 --> 00:36:08,640
The shock makes everything go quiet for a moment.

610
00:36:08,641 --> 00:36:12,120
You have to be able to extinguish this sort of chaotic activity

611
00:36:12,121 --> 00:36:13,720
of the individual cells.

612
00:36:13,721 --> 00:36:16,000
And then once you've extinguished it at all,

613
00:36:16,001 --> 00:36:19,740
then the normal conduction system of the heart

614
00:36:19,741 --> 00:36:26,120
can allow the heart to get back to the periodic coordinated

615
00:36:26,121 --> 00:36:28,680
pumping that you need to have.

616
00:36:28,681 --> 00:36:30,560
Think of it as rebooting your computer.

617
00:36:33,560 --> 00:36:36,640
Physio Control in Redmond, Washington,

618
00:36:36,641 --> 00:36:39,240
is a leading manufacturer of external defibrillators.

619
00:36:42,040 --> 00:36:45,500
It's about 2,000 volts at about 40 amps of current

620
00:36:45,501 --> 00:36:48,040
for the typical person.

621
00:36:48,041 --> 00:36:52,440
40 amps is the amount of current it would take to run,

622
00:36:52,441 --> 00:36:54,620
say, your washer or dryer.

623
00:36:54,621 --> 00:36:57,320
Because it's external energy, there

624
00:36:57,321 --> 00:37:01,700
are all kinds of issues related to the chest wall, its thickness,

625
00:37:01,701 --> 00:37:05,180
how muscular it is, the ribs, because you're

626
00:37:05,181 --> 00:37:08,140
going through that tissue to get to the heart.

627
00:37:08,141 --> 00:37:11,960
And so the first shock may just be dealing with what's

628
00:37:11,961 --> 00:37:14,380
preventing the electricity to get to the heart.

629
00:37:14,381 --> 00:37:18,260
And then the second shock may be the shock that actually works.

630
00:37:18,261 --> 00:37:20,480
Resume CPR, please.

631
00:37:20,481 --> 00:37:24,280
Some studies show that survival rates are as high as 74%

632
00:37:24,281 --> 00:37:27,280
when defibrillation is given within three minutes of collapse.

633
00:37:28,360 --> 00:37:30,760
Hold CPR. Is there a pulse?

634
00:37:31,040 --> 00:37:31,800
We got a pulse.

635
00:37:32,680 --> 00:37:33,760
Great job, guys.

636
00:37:34,100 --> 00:37:37,420
When you need a shock, it is the only thing that will help you.

637
00:37:37,720 --> 00:37:40,680
Because when you need a shock, you don't need it 20 minutes from now.

638
00:37:40,940 --> 00:37:43,200
You don't need it 10 minutes from now. You need it now.

639
00:37:43,201 --> 00:37:49,400
You never, ever get tired of hearing about someone's life being saved.

640
00:37:50,420 --> 00:37:51,180
Never.

641
00:37:51,181 --> 00:37:57,060
Neither defibrillation nor ECT are more than a century old.

642
00:37:58,020 --> 00:38:01,920
But the true origins of medical shock date back to an ancient world

643
00:38:01,921 --> 00:38:06,780
and a strange genus of fish with genuine shocking power.

644
00:38:09,980 --> 00:38:14,560
Chances of surviving cardiac arrest diminish 10% as every minute passes.

645
00:38:15,320 --> 00:38:18,440
So what happens when the victim is flying at 30,000 feet?

646
00:38:18,441 --> 00:38:23,300
United States federal law requires airliners to carry an external defibrillator,

647
00:38:23,860 --> 00:38:28,720
leading to a 40% survival rate increase for cardiac victims caught in transit.

648
00:38:29,460 --> 00:38:32,360
Most shocking will return on Modern Marvels.

649
00:38:36,720 --> 00:38:39,600
When your car battery dies, you replace it.

650
00:38:41,180 --> 00:38:43,680
The same goes for the batteries in your flashlight.

651
00:38:43,681 --> 00:38:49,680
But what about the small electric wiring inside of you?

652
00:38:49,681 --> 00:38:56,680
Biobatteries, microscopic, implantable, and all natural power sources for your eyes and

653
00:38:56,681 --> 00:38:59,680
ears may sound futuristic.

654
00:38:59,681 --> 00:39:02,680
In fact, for now, they are.

655
00:39:02,681 --> 00:39:08,680
But the key to unlocking their secret may be close, lying in wait in the murky waters

656
00:39:08,681 --> 00:39:11,680
of the Amazon.

657
00:39:11,681 --> 00:39:17,900
Welcome to the shocking potential of Electrophorus electricus, the electric eel.

658
00:39:18,800 --> 00:39:24,580
He has no scales, no teeth, but if you don't show him respect, he will kill you.

659
00:39:25,520 --> 00:39:28,680
Just ask this caiman as it nears a high voltage end.

660
00:39:30,840 --> 00:39:38,140
A solitary nocturnal hunter, the electric eel can deliver a jolt of more than 600 volts at 1.5 amps.

661
00:39:38,141 --> 00:39:41,760
This guy's only six years old.

662
00:39:41,880 --> 00:39:46,060
They live past 20, and they can go from 40 to 60 pounds

663
00:39:46,061 --> 00:39:47,680
and grow to 9 foot.

664
00:39:47,920 --> 00:39:51,780
So you can imagine an animal that will shock anything to death.

665
00:39:52,920 --> 00:39:56,320
That kind of punch earns the eel the rank of an apex predator,

666
00:39:57,000 --> 00:39:58,000
top of the food chain.

667
00:39:59,220 --> 00:40:01,140
It'll eat birds, it'll eat fish,

668
00:40:01,540 --> 00:40:03,700
it'll eat pretty much anything it can get in its mouth.

669
00:40:03,701 --> 00:40:11,200
To understand the eel's shocking power is to understand its remarkable physiology.

670
00:40:12,700 --> 00:40:22,320
This is an electric eel collected in 1936 from the upper Amazon, Peru, and it has all the qualities

671
00:40:22,321 --> 00:40:26,297
that a shocking electric eel clearly demonstrates.

672
00:40:26,298 --> 00:40:29,320
It's got a head, a medium-sized mouth,

673
00:40:29,321 --> 00:40:30,760
little pectoral fins.

674
00:40:31,480 --> 00:40:33,540
Its anus is located right here,

675
00:40:34,120 --> 00:40:37,320
about 20% behind the tip of the snout.

676
00:40:37,840 --> 00:40:41,700
And the rest of it, 80% of this fish,

677
00:40:42,240 --> 00:40:43,420
is nothing but batteries.

678
00:40:44,780 --> 00:40:47,320
Electric eels possess three pairs of organs

679
00:40:47,321 --> 00:40:50,000
that generate differing levels of electric charge

680
00:40:50,001 --> 00:40:51,780
in order to meet various needs.

681
00:40:54,100 --> 00:40:56,900
The Sachs organ functions like sonar,

682
00:40:56,901 --> 00:40:59,740
generating low levels of electricity that orient the eel

683
00:40:59,840 --> 00:41:00,640
as it swims.

684
00:41:03,640 --> 00:41:05,940
It's a snapshot of the electrical field around them

685
00:41:06,040 --> 00:41:07,140
of what they are.

686
00:41:07,240 --> 00:41:08,640
Maybe there's a trunk over here.

687
00:41:08,740 --> 00:41:10,640
There's a rock over here.

688
00:41:10,740 --> 00:41:12,540
And as they're gliding through the water,

689
00:41:12,640 --> 00:41:14,060
they're sensing this and picking it up.

690
00:41:14,160 --> 00:41:16,760
Once that picks up something that they may think of as food,

691
00:41:16,860 --> 00:41:18,160
they'll head towards that.

692
00:41:18,260 --> 00:41:20,460
Their amperage from their hunter's organ or their main

693
00:41:20,560 --> 00:41:22,160
organ can now come into play.

694
00:41:24,960 --> 00:41:26,900
As the eel nears its prey,

695
00:41:26,901 --> 00:41:32,140
its brain initiates a chemical reaction within the cells of the hunters and main

696
00:41:32,141 --> 00:41:40,100
organs. These organs are composed of thousands of small flat cells known as

697
00:41:40,101 --> 00:41:46,120
electrocytes that are stacked in a sequence much like a dry cell battery.

698
00:41:46,121 --> 00:41:53,800
When energized each electrocyte generates a minuscule 0.15 volts but line them up

699
00:41:53,801 --> 00:41:55,200
and fire them at the same time.

700
00:41:55,700 --> 00:41:59,020
And the collective jolt is magnified to 600 volts.

701
00:42:00,960 --> 00:42:03,220
So as they get closer to this object,

702
00:42:03,360 --> 00:42:04,480
they may be amplifying it,

703
00:42:04,760 --> 00:42:06,160
and then if it really is food, zap,

704
00:42:06,240 --> 00:42:08,760
you know, they'll go for it, and then that's it.

705
00:42:09,680 --> 00:42:12,480
The eel focuses the electric charge through its head.

706
00:42:14,000 --> 00:42:16,080
And if you think that's comfortable for the eel,

707
00:42:16,520 --> 00:42:17,380
well, think again.

708
00:42:17,660 --> 00:42:20,140
When you look closely, when we feed this fish,

709
00:42:20,600 --> 00:42:22,520
you can see it jolt its prey,

710
00:42:22,521 --> 00:42:24,140
and you can also see it cringe.

711
00:42:24,300 --> 00:42:27,440
It does that because it shocks itself,

712
00:42:27,480 --> 00:42:29,200
and its brain is feeling that.

713
00:42:29,520 --> 00:42:34,320
So its brain is protected by a fatty layer and cartilage

714
00:42:34,321 --> 00:42:36,860
so that it doesn't have the same problems

715
00:42:36,861 --> 00:42:41,080
that the prey item has when it jolts it and electrifies it.

716
00:42:42,100 --> 00:42:44,880
Throughout the world, there are more than 500 species

717
00:42:44,881 --> 00:42:46,460
of fish that generate electricity,

718
00:42:47,580 --> 00:42:49,660
including the Mediterranean torpedo fish.

719
00:42:49,661 --> 00:42:56,100
The torpedoes are one of the four families of electric rays.

720
00:42:56,460 --> 00:43:00,460
They're also called numb fish or cramp fish,

721
00:43:00,780 --> 00:43:02,860
and they certainly do numb their prey.

722
00:43:02,861 --> 00:43:05,820
It lands above its prey at night

723
00:43:05,821 --> 00:43:08,260
and then wraps its fins around it

724
00:43:08,261 --> 00:43:12,200
and at the same time zzzz and stuns its prey.

725
00:43:13,820 --> 00:43:16,700
But no shocking fish rivals the electric eel.

726
00:43:16,701 --> 00:43:21,200
You'll see the amount of abilities the fish has

727
00:43:21,300 --> 00:43:24,140
to suck down something a little larger than itself.

728
00:43:24,240 --> 00:43:27,820
And again, this fish will eat almost every day,

729
00:43:27,920 --> 00:43:31,520
because it's a, ooh, OK, well, I got that.

730
00:43:34,980 --> 00:43:38,460
Even indirect contact leads to a good shock.

731
00:43:38,560 --> 00:43:39,460
I got it.

732
00:43:39,560 --> 00:43:40,660
Yeah, my finger was in the water.

733
00:43:40,760 --> 00:43:43,820
So yeah, I got it a little bit.

734
00:43:43,920 --> 00:43:46,700
Yeah, my heart's racing.

735
00:43:46,701 --> 00:43:50,700
Today's momentary shock, like the one from this electric eel,

736
00:43:50,701 --> 00:43:54,700
may well hold the key to tomorrow's latest biomedical advances.

737
00:43:56,700 --> 00:44:00,700
Perhaps one day, jump-starting our eyes and ears,

738
00:44:00,701 --> 00:44:03,700
it seems surprising that something so dangerous

739
00:44:03,701 --> 00:44:06,700
can also prove to be so valuable.

740
00:44:08,700 --> 00:44:11,700
But that's true of nearly everything about electric shock,

741
00:44:11,701 --> 00:44:13,700
negative and positive.

